{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"kind\":\"final\",\"decision\":\"answer\",\"answer\":1220,\"evidence\":[\"receipt-1\"],\"confidence\":0.9,\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"reason\":\"Dynamic programming counting binary strings of length 14 avoiding 000 and 111\"}","reasoning_content":"We need count binary strings length 14 that avoid forbidden substrings \"111\" and \"000\". So no three consecutive same bits. Count sequences of length n with no run of 3 identical bits. This is classic recurrence. Let a_n be number of sequences length n ending with run of length 1 of some bit, b_n ending with run of length 2. For each bit type? Actually we can consider two states: last run length 1, last run length 2. For each state, we don't care which bit. Transition: from run length1, we can add same bit to make run length2, or add opposite bit to start new run length1. From run length2, we can only add opposite bit to start run length1 (since adding same would make length3 forbidden). So recurrence: Let A_n = number ending with run length1, B_n = ending with run length2. For n=1: A1=2 (either 0 or 1), B1=0. For n>1: A_{n} = A_{n-1} + B_{n-1} (adding opposite bit to any previous run). B_{n} = A_{n-1} (adding same bit to run length1). So we can compute. Let's compute quickly.\n\nn=1: A1=2, B1=0, total T1=2\nn=2: A2 = A1+B1 =2+0=2; B2 = A1=2; T2=4\nn=3: A3 = A2+B2 =2+2=4; B3 = A2=2; T3=6\nn=4: A4 = A3+B3 =4+2=6; B4 = A3=4; T4=10\nn=5: A5 = A4+B4 =6+4=10; B5 = A4=6; T5=16\nn=6: A6 =10+6=16; B6=10; T6=26\nn=7: A7=16+10=26; B7=16; T7=42\nn=8: A8=26+16=42; B8=26; T8=68\nn=9: A9=42+26=68; B9=42; T9=110\nn=10: A10=68+42=110; B10=68; T10=178\nn=11: A11=110+68=178; B11=110; T11=288\nn=12: A12=178+110=288; B12=178; T12=466\nn=13: A13=288+178=466; B13=288; T13=754\nn=14: A14=466+288=754; B14=466; T14=1220\n\nThus answer 1220. Provide final JSON."}}],"created":1790984100,"model":"C:\\Users\\d2j3\\Documents\\Codex\\2026-09-10\\napraw\\outputs\\m